atus model derives — the existing dashboard already provides this state, so\n64\tparallel-with-merge-coordination is defensible, but the coupling could have been called out\n65\texplicitly in E002's risks.\n66\t\n67\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n68\tComponent/team tuples are cleanly separated: Dashboard UI (E001–E004, E007), UXD (E005), Content\n69\t(E006, mapped to canonical component UXD with documented rationale since no Content component\n70\texists), Docs (E008). No epic spans multiple teams. Work-product types are properly separated —\n71\tcode implementation (E001–E004), design authoring (E005), content authoring (E006), integration\n72\t(E007), docs (E008). The E003 (placeholder) / E007 (final treatment) split is not duplication:\n73\tE007 does not rebuild E003's surfaces, it swaps placeholder treatments for finalized deliverables —\n74\tthe shape mandated by the strategy's explicit non-blocking-UX directive. No epic exceeds ~2 weeks.\n75\tMinor observation (no score impact): the RHOAIUX-7470 status-message content review appears as an\n76\tacceptance criterion in both E005 (\"The status message content review is completed\") and E006\n77\t(\"The copy is reviewed with UXD\") — duplicated accountability for one joint review milestone;\n78\townership should be assigned to one epic with the other referencing it.\n79\t\n80\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n81\tAll eight epics are Implementation and all produce concrete artifacts (status model code, API\n82\tclient, UI indicators, form hardening, design specs, microcopy lookup table, integration changes,\n83\tpublished docs). No Investigation epics — correctly so: the RBAC open question does not change\n84\twhich epics exist or what they build (403 handling is unconditional, modeled as an AC plus a health\n85\twarning), and the indicator-form question is a design deliverable owned by an impacted team inside\n86\tthe strategy, modeled as a parallel Implementation epic. All `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields\n8