{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b8e6cfe1e608fec","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8002\n3\ttitle: Integrate Kueue Workload Scheduling Information in Workbenches Overview\n4\tstatus: Release Pending\n5\tpriority: Major\n6\tlabels:\n7\t- GA\n8\t- GPU-as-a-Service\n9\t- dashboard-tangerine-scrum\n10\t- nse_workflow:roadmap\n11\t- nse_workflow:strat\n12\t- refined\n13\tlinks:\n14\t- 'related-strategy: RHAISTRAT-7432'\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## Summary\n18\t\n19\tExtend the Red Hat OpenShift AI workbenches overview UI to display Kueue workload scheduling information, providing platform operators and data scientists with comprehensive visibility into workload queuing, resource allocation, and scheduling status.\n20\t\n21\t## Background\n22\t\n23\tThe current workbenches overview displays basic workbench information including name, container image, size, and runtime status. However, when Kueue is deployed for workload management, users lack visibility into:\n24\t\n25\t* Queue assignment and priority\n26\t* Resource quotas and consumption\n27\t* Workload scheduling status and position in queue\n28\t* Wait times and resource availability\n29\t* Queue-specific policies and limits\n30\t\n31\t[Refinement Doc]([internal link removed])\n32\t\n33\tBrainstorming: [GPUaaS Miro ]([internal link removed])\n34\t\n35\t[UXD Diagramming]([internal link removed])\n36\t\n37\t## Problem Statement\n38\t\n39\tData scientists and platform operators need better visibility into workload scheduling when Kueue is managing distributed workloads. The current UI provides no indication of:\n40\t\n41\t1. **Queue Status**: Which queue a workbench is assigned to\n42\t2. **Scheduling State**: Whether a workload is pending, admitted, or running\n43\t3. **Resource Constraints**: Available vs. requested resources\n44\t4. **Wait Information**: Position in queue and estimated wait time\n45\t5. **Policy Context**: Priority classes and preemption policies\n46\t\n47\t## Proposed Solution\n48\t\n49\t### **Core Requirements**\n50\t\n51\t1. **Conditional Display**: Only show Kueue information when Kueue is deployed and enabled\n52\t2. **Queue Information Column**: Add queue name and status\n53\t3. **Scheduling Status**: Display workload admission status\n54\t4. **Resource Visibility**: Show requested vs. available resources\n55\t5. **Wait Time Indicators**: Queue position and estimated scheduling time\n56\t\n57\t### **UI Enhancements**\n58\t\n59\t#### **New Columns (when Kueue enabled)**\n60\t\n61\t* **Queue**: Display assigned queue name with status indicator\n62\t* **Scheduling Status**: Admitted | Pending | Running | Suspended\n63\t* **Queue Position**: Position in queue (for pending workloads)\n64\t* **Resources**: Requested vs. available (CPU/GPU/Memory)\n65\t\n66\t#### **Status Indicators**\n67\t\n68\t* 🟢 **Admitted**: Workload has been admitted and resources allocated\n69\t* 🟡 **Pending**: Workload is queued waiting for resources\n70\t* 🔵 **Running**: Workload is actively running\n71\t* 🟠 **Suspended**: Workload has been suspended due to policy\n72\t* ⚫ **No Queue**: Workbench not managed by Kueue\n73\t\n74\t#### **Detailed View Enhancements**\n75\t\n76\t* **Queue Details Panel**: Click to expand queue information\n77\t* **Resource Breakdown**: Detailed resource allocation view\n78\t* **Policy Information**: Priority class and preemption settings\n79\t* **Historical Data**: Previous queue wait times and patterns\n80\t\n81\t## User Stories\n82\t\n83\t### **As a Data Scientist**\n84\t\n85\t* I want to see if my workbench is queued so I understand why it's not starting\n86\t* I want to know my position in the queue and estimated wait time\n87\t* I want to understand which queue my workbench is assigned to\n88\t\n89\t### **As a Platform Operator**\n90\t\n91\t* I want to see queue utilization across all workbenches\n92\t* I want to identify resource bottlenecks and scheduling issues\n93\t* I want to monitor queue policies and their effectiveness\n94\t\n95\t### **As a Team Lead**\n96\t\n97\t* I want to see my team's workbenches across different priority queues\n98\t* I want to understand resource allocation and usage patterns\n99\t* I want to optimize queue assignments for better throughput\n100\t\n101\t## Success Criteria\n102\t\n103\t1. **Visibility**: Users can see queue status for all workbenches\n104\t2. **Performance**: UI remains responsive with Kueue integration\n105\t3. **Usability**: Clear indicators when Kueue is not available\n106\t4. **Scalability**: Handles large numbers of queued workloads\n107\t5. **Integration**: Seamless experience with existing workbench workflows\n108\t\n109\t## Strategy (AI Generated by Agentic SDLC Pipeline)\n110\t\n111\t> **Note:** The full strategy exceeds Jira's description size limit and is stored as an attachment: `RHAISTRAT-8002-strategy.md`. The TL;DR is shown below for quick reference.\n112\t\n113\t### TL;DR\n114\t\n115\tImprove Kueue workload scheduling visibility in the odh-dashboard: fix the status model to distinguish Preempted from other eviction reasons, integrate the Kueue Visibility API for queue position data, add a positive Kueue indicator on project details, surface anomaly indicators for non-Kueue workloads in Kueue-managed namespaces, and harden the HardwareProfile form with LocalQueue name validation and GitOps-managed notebook support.\n116\t\n117\t**Components**: odh-dashboard\n118\t\n119\t## Staff Engineer / SME Input\n120\t\n121\t**Components**: odh-dashboard\n122\t\n123\tWork required here is only related to the odh-dashboard component (frontend). The rhods-operator's HardwareProfile mutating webhook handles injecting the `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name` label onto Notebook CRs — that mechanism is not part of this STRAT. Remove rhods-operator from the Affected Components table.\n124\t\n125\t**Scope Constraints**\n126\t\n127\tThis STRAT covers Kueue workload scheduling visibility in the workbenches overview only. The following are explicitly out of scope:\n128\t\n129\t* Model deployments Kueue integration (covered by RHAISTRAT-7432)\n130\t* Rich queue data visualization / \"Kueue Insights\" consolidated admin view (future work)\n131\t* Queue capacity/quota preview at the LocalQueue level — this is not feasible because LocalQueues do not carry quota information. Quota lives at the ClusterQueue level, which may be fed by multiple LocalQueues, and data scientists may not have RBAC to read ClusterQueues. We cannot reliably calculate available capacity for a given user.\n132\t* Changes to the Kueue feature flag behavior (collapsing the four-flag activation model into fewer signals is desirable but is a separate effort)\n133\t* Strict opt-in/opt-out controls for Kueue at the project or workload level — for now, dashboard remains opinionated (auto-enables Kueue) but must explicitly tell users what it is doing rather than implicitly forcing behavior\n134\t\n135\t**Architecture Constraints**\n136\t\n137\tAll K8s API calls from the dashboard use the logged-in user's token via the `/api/k8s` passthrough proxy. The flow is: Browser → Frontend SDK → `/api/k8s` (backend proxy) → K8s API (with user token from `x-forwarded-access-token` header). Every API call is subject to the user's RBAC permissions.\n138\t\n139\tThe namespace management model is **QueueNameRequired** mode (the default in RHBoK). Only workloads carrying the `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name` label are managed by Kueue. Workloads without this label run normally, bypassing Kueue entirely. The dashboard should warn about missing queue labels but never block workload creation because of them.\n140\t\n141\tWorkload-to-Notebook matching uses `buildWorkloadMapForNotebooks()` in `api/k8s/workloads.ts`. It checks (in order): the `kueue.x-k8s.io/job-name` label on the Workload matching the notebook name, then `ownerReferences` on the Workload pointing to a Job, Notebook, StatefulSet, or Pod with a matching name.\n142\t\n143\tQueue position data is NOT a field on the Workload object. It lives on a separate type called `PendingWorkload`, served by Kueue's Visibility API — an aggregated API server at API group `visibility.kueue.x-k8s.io`. The `VisibilityOnDemand` feature gate is Beta and enabled by default since Kueue v0.9. The position data is exposed as subresources on queues:\n144\t\n145\t* LocalQueue (namespaced): `GET /apis/visibility.kueue.x-k8s.io/v1beta2/namespaces/{ns}/localqueues/{name}/pendingworkloads`\n146\t* ClusterQueue (cluster-scoped, admin only): `GET /apis/visibility.kueue.x-k8s.io/v1beta2/clusterqueues/{name}/pendingworkloads`\n147\t\n148\tRBAC for LocalQueue position data: Kueue ships a `pending-workloads-lq-viewer-role` ClusterRole that is aggregated to `batch-user`, `view`, `admin`, and `edit` standard Kubernetes roles. If RHOAI binds users to any of these roles (which is standard), position data is available without additional RBAC configuration.\n149\t\n150\tA 403 on the Visibility API must fail silently — position info is supplementary, not critical.\n151\t\n152\tThe status model must be state-driven from Workload CR conditions and pod/container statuses — not event-driven from hardcoded progress steps. The approach should: provide human-friendly override messages for known condition/reason combinations, but always fall back to showing the raw Kueue status message rather than showing nothing. Under no circumstance should the UI show no information when a Kueue condition exists — a Kubernetes error message that is less friendly is always better than a blank state.\n153\t\n154\tThe current dashboard status model conflates all Evicted conditions as \"Preempted.\" This is incorrect. Eviction reasons include: Preempted, PodsReadyTimeout, AdmissionCheck, ClusterQueueStopped, LocalQueueStopped, Deactivated, NodeFailures, MaximumExecutionTimeExceeded. The dashboard must distinguish preemption from other eviction reasons. At minimum, separate \"Preempted\" (higher-priority workload took resources) from \"Evicted\" (admin action or timeout).\n155\t\n156\tThe `WorkloadCondition` type union in `k8sTypes.ts` is missing several condition types. The `'Failed'` entry in the union is incorrect — there is no condition type called `Failed` in Kueue. Failure is expressed as `Finished` with reason `Failed`. The type should include: `QuotaReserved`, `Admitted`, `PodsReady`, `Finished`, `Evicted`, `Preempted`, `Requeued`, `BlockedOnPreemptionGates`, `DeactivationTarget`.\n157\t\n158\t**UI Direction**\n159\t\n160\t_Project details page_: Add a positive indicator when the namespace has the `kueue.openshift.io/managed=true` label. The exact form (info banner, badge, label) needs UX input (RHOAIUX-7470), but the requirement is non-negotiable: users must know their project uses queue-based scheduling because it fundamentally affects how their workloads start. This is not \"showing Kubernetes stuff\" — it is answering the question a user will inevitably ask when their workload doesn't start immediately.\n161\t\n162\t_Workload-level Kueue indicators_: Do NOT show \"managed by Kueue\" on every workload in a Kueue-managed namespace. That is redundant in the steady state. Instead, only flag the anomaly: if a namespace IS Kueue-managed but a specific workload is NOT Kueue-managed (missing queue label), show a visual indicator on that workload. The only way this happens through dashboard is if the user bypasses dashboard via GitOps or CLI. So this indicator serves the hybrid GitOps + dashboard user.\n163\t\n164\t_HardwareProfile dropdown_: Keep strict filtering behavior (only show queue-based HPs in Kueue-managed namespaces). But add an explicit indication of WHY the dropdown contains what it does — an info icon or tooltip that explains \"Only queue-enabled hardware profiles are shown because this project uses queue-based scheduling.\" The principle is: be opinionated, but tell users we are being opinionated.\n165\t\n166\t_HP details popover for GitOps-managed notebooks_: A notebook can have `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name` applied directly via GitOps without going through a HardwareProfile. RHBoK documentation encourages applying queue labels directly — this is a supported pattern. The HP details popover must handle this case: when no HP scheduling data exists, fall back to reading the queue label from the notebook's own labels. Resolve the cluster queue name from the project's LocalQueue list. Indicate the source: \"Queue: my-queue (applied directly)\" vs. \"Queue: my-queue (via hardware profile).\"\n167\t\n168\t_LocalQueue name validation_: The HardwareProfile admin form must validate LocalQueue names against `k8sNameRegex` (`/^[a-z0-9]([-a-z0-9]*[a-z0-9])?$/`). We cannot validate that the queue actually exists because HardwareProfiles are cluster-scoped while LocalQueues are namespace-scoped — at HP creation time we don't know the target namespace. At workbench creation time, if the specified local queue does not exist in the namespace, display a warning — NOT an error. The admin may have intended for the LQ to be created later, or it may be coming via GitOps. These are eventual consistent systems — Kueue itself will report the error via the Workload's conditions if the queue truly doesn't exist.\n169\t\n170\t_Status messages_: Provide human-friendly message overrides for known condition/reason combinations as a lookup table. The fallback is always the raw Kueue condition message. The override table is a content/UX deliverable (RHOAIUX-7470), but engineering should not be blocked on it — use reasonable defaults and replace when content review is complete.\n171\t\n172\t**Impacted Teams**\n173\t\n174\tThe following is a complete list of Impacted Teams:\n175\t\n176\t* | Dashboard UI | odh-dashboard frontend | Primary implementor: status model, Visibility API integration, UI indicators, form validation |\n177\t* | UXD | Design system | Visual treatment for positive Kueue indicator, anomaly indicator design, status message content review (RHOAIUX-7470) |\n178\t* | Content | Microcopy | Status message wording for Kueue conditions — human-readable overrides for known condition/reason combos (RHOAIUX-7470) |\n179\t* | Docs | Documentation | At minimum a release note; determine if feature-level documentation is warranted |\n180\t\n181\tNo other teams are impacted by this work.\n182\t\n183\t**High Level Requirements**\n184\t\n185\tThe following is the complete list of High Level Requirements we want to commit to — no other high level requirements should be mentioned in the document:\n186\t\n187\t* \\[P0\\] Distinguish Preempted from other Eviction reasons (ClusterQueueStopped, LocalQueueStopped, PodsReadyTimeout, Deactivated, etc.) in the workbench status display\n188\t* \\[P0\\] Surface Requeued state with retry count and next retry time from `requeueState` when a workload is evicted and waiting to re-enter the queue\n189\t* \\[P0\\] Add positive Kueue indicator on project details page when namespace has `kueue.openshift.io/managed=true` label\n190\t* \\[P0\\] Add anomaly indicator on workloads in a Kueue-managed namespace that are NOT managed by Kueue (missing `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name` label)\n191\t* \\[P0\\] Display workload scheduling status derived from Workload CR conditions with human-friendly message overrides and raw message fallback\n192\t* \\[P1\\] Integrate Kueue Visibility API for queue position display (`positionInLocalQueue`, `positionInClusterQueue`) via `PendingWorkloadsSummary` subresource on LocalQueue — gracefully degrade on 403\n193\t* \\[P1\\] Fix HardwareProfile details popover for GitOps-managed notebooks: read queue label directly when no HP match exists, indicate source\n194\t* \\[P1\\] Add `k8sNameRegex` validation to LocalQueue name field in HardwareProfile admin form\n195\t* \\[P1\\] Add explicit info indicator on HP dropdown explaining why only queue-enabled profiles are shown in Kueue-managed namespaces\n196\t* \\[P2\\] Improve status model progress steps to ensure all information related to workload scheduling is surfaced — move away from wildcard/substring matching of events to a structured, extensible status derivation model\n197\t* \\[P2\\] Improve status message granularity: distinguish WaitForRecovery from WaitForStart, surface AdmissionCheck pending/retry states\n198\t\n199\t**Dependencies**\n200\t\n201\tThe only actual dependency is around UXD finalized designs for the positive Kueue indicator and status message content (RHOAIUX-7470). However, development must NOT be blocked on UX answers. The UX questions are about microcopy and visual treatment, not major redesigns. Use placeholder copy during development and replace when UX delivers.\n202\t\n203\tAny other dependency presently listed should be removed or moved to Assumptions.\n204\t\n205\t**Non-Functional Requirements**\n206\t\n207\tThe following is a complete list of criteria we want defined as \"Non-Functional Requirements\":\n208\t\n209\t* **Backwards Compatibility**: All changes are purely additive and conditional. Existing workbench behavior is unchanged when Kueue is not enabled or namespace is not Kueue-managed.\n210\t* **Backwards Compatibility**: The status model must handle Workload CRs that contain condition types not yet in the dashboard's `WorkloadCondition` type union. Unknown conditions must be shown with raw message text, never silently dropped.\n211\t* **Graceful Degradation**: A 403 on the Visibility API (queue position) must fail silently — position info is supplementary. Use the existing `getGenericErrorCode` pattern from `DistributedWorkloadsContext.tsx`.\n212\t* **Graceful Degradation**: If a Workload CR has a condition/reason combination not covered by human-friendly message overrides, display the raw Kueue status message from the condition's `.message` field. Never show a blank state.\n213\t\n214\t**Out of Scope**\n215\t\n216\tThe following is a complete list of functionality we want defined as \"Out of Scope\":\n217\t\n218\t* Model deployments Kueue integration (RHAISTRAT-7432)\n219\t* Queue capacity/quota preview at the LocalQueue level — LocalQueues do not carry quota information, and users may not have RBAC to read ClusterQueues\n220\t* Rich queue data visualization / \"Kueue Insights\" consolidated admin view\n221\t* Estimated wait time predictions — Kueue does not provide this natively; only factual elapsed wait time is displayed\n222\t* Changes to the Kueue feature flag activation model (collapsing the four-flag model)\n223\t* Strict opt-in/opt-out controls for Kueue at the project or workload level\n224\t* Queue management actions from workbenches UI — this STRAT is read-only visibility\n225\t* Workload types other than notebooks (training, serving, Ray)\n226\t\n227\t**Acceptance Criteria**\n228\t\n229\tThe following is a complete list of criteria we want defined as \"Acceptance Criteria\". This list should be considered definitive and validated:\n230\t\n231\t* Given a Kueue-managed namespace where a workbench has been evicted due to `ClusterQueueStopped`, when the user views the workbench status, then the status displays \"Evicted\" with reason \"Queue stopped by administrator\" — NOT \"Preempted\", measured by the status label text and variant differing from the Preempted state.\n232\t\n233\t* Given a workbench has been evicted and the Workload CR has a `Requeued` condition with `requeueState.requeueAt` set, when the user views the workbench status, then the status displays the retry information including next retry time and attempt count, measured by the status text including the computed retry time from `requeueState`.\n234\t\n235\t* Given a Kueue-managed namespace, when the user views the project details page, then a positive indicator is visible stating this project uses queue-based scheduling, measured by the indicator element being present in the DOM when the namespace label `kueue.openshift.io/managed=true` exists and absent when it does not.\n236\t\n237\t* Given a Kueue-managed namespace containing a workbench that does NOT have the `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name` label (created via GitOps or CLI without queue assignment), when the user views the workbenches overview, then that specific workbench displays a visual indicator that it is not managed by Kueue, measured by the anomaly indicator being present only on non-Kueue workloads and absent on Kueue-managed workloads.\n238\t\n239\t* Given a workbench is in QueuingPending state and the user's token has `batch-user` RBAC to the Visibility API, when the workbenches overview page renders, then the queue position is displayed (position in local queue), measured by the position number being present and matching the value from the `pendingworkloads` subresource.\n240\t\n241\t* Given a workbench is in QueuingPending state and the user's token does NOT have RBAC to the Visibility API, when the workbenches overview page renders, then the queue position is silently omitted with no error displayed, measured by the absence of both position data and error indicators.\n242\t\n243\t* Given a GitOps-managed workbench with `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name` label applied directly (no matching HardwareProfile), when the user opens the HardwareProfile details popover, then the popover displays the queue name with indication it was applied directly (not via hardware profile), measured by the popover text including the queue name and a source indicator.\n244\t\n245\t* Given an admin is creating a HardwareProfile and enters an invalid LocalQueue name (e.g., contains uppercase or spaces), when the form validates, then a validation error is shown for the LocalQueue name field, measured by the form displaying an inline error referencing the Kubernetes name format.\n246\t\n247\t* Given a workbench is being created with a HardwareProfile referencing a LocalQueue that does not exist in the target namespace, when the creation form is submitted, then a warning (not error) is displayed indicating the local queue was not found, measured by the warning being dismissible and not blocking workbench creation.\n248\t\n249\t* Given a workbench's Workload CR has a condition type or reason not recognized by the dashboard's status model, when the workbench status is rendered, then the raw Kueue condition message is displayed rather than a blank state, measured by the status text containing the Workload CR's condition `.message` field content.\n250\t\n251\t* Given a workbench is in a Kueue-managed namespace and the user opens the workbench creation form, when the HardwareProfile dropdown renders with only queue-enabled profiles, then an info indicator explains why only queue-enabled profiles are shown, measured by the presence of a tooltip or info icon adjacent to the dropdown.\n252\t\n253\t**Effort Estimate**\n254\t\n255\tEffort estimate should be **S (Small, 1-3 sprints)**\n256\t\n257\tThe work is bounded to odh-dashboard frontend changes: status model improvements, Visibility API integration, UI indicators, and form validation. No new backend services, no controller changes, no new CRDs. Development should start immediately and not be blocked on UX answers — use placeholder copy and replace when UX delivers.\n258\t\n259\t**Risks**\n260\t\n261\tThe following is a complete list of table rows we want defined as \"Risks\":\n262\t\n263\t| Visibility API RBAC may not be granted to data scientists in all RHOAI clusters | Queue position data unavailable to regular users | Code handles 403 gracefully — position info is omitted silently. Validate whether `batch-user` RBAC is present by default in RHOAI clusters. If not, position display becomes admin-only. |\n264\t| --- | --- | --- |\n265\t\n266\t| Status message content may not resonate with non-Kubernetes users | Poor UX — raw Kueue condition messages are unintelligible to data scientists | Provide human-friendly message overrides for all known condition/reason combos. Content review with UX/Content team (RHOAIUX-7470). Always show something rather than nothing. |\n267\t| --- | --- | --- |\n268\t\n269\t| Hybrid GitOps + dashboard users create workloads that don't match dashboard assumptions | Confusing state where some workloads in a Kueue-managed namespace are not Kueue-managed | Surface the anomaly indicator for non-Kueue workloads in Kueue-managed namespaces. Dashboard cannot prevent GitOps-created workloads from bypassing Kueue. |\n270\t| --- | --- | --- |\n271\t\n272\t**Assumptions**\n273\t\n274\tThe following is a complete list of items we want defined as \"Assumptions\":\n275\t\n276\t* Kueue's Visibility API (`visibility.kueue.x-k8s.io/v1beta2`) is available in RHBoK shipped with RHOAI 3.5. The `VisibilityOnDemand` feature gate is Beta and enabled by default since Kueue v0.9. The `pending-workloads-lq-viewer-role` ClusterRole is aggregated to `batch-user`, `view`, `admin`, and `edit` roles — **confirmed**\n277\t* The `WorkloadCondition` type union in `k8sTypes.ts` can be extended to include `Requeued`, `BlockedOnPreemptionGates`, and other condition types without breaking existing status logic\n278\t* RHBoK operates in QueueNameRequired mode (the default) — workloads without the queue label bypass Kueue entirely\n279\t\n280\t**Open Questions**\n281\t\n282\tThe ONLY open questions we should leave in the STRAT are:\n283\t\n284\t* | Do data scientists in RHOAI clusters receive Kubernetes `view` or `edit` ClusterRole bindings that would aggregate the `pending-workloads-lq-viewer-role`? | Kueue's `pending-workloads-lq-viewer-role` is aggregated to `batch-user`, `view`, `admin`, and `edit` standard Kubernetes roles. If RHOAI binds users to any of these roles, position data is available automatically. Code handles 403 gracefully either way, but affects whether we invest in position UI polish. | AI Core Platform |\n285\t* | What form should the positive Kueue indicator on the project details page take? | UX decision on info banner vs. badge vs. label. Engineering effort is minimal regardless of choice. | UXD (RHOAIUX-7470) |\n286\t\n287\t**Prerequisites & Process Gates**\n288\t\n289\tDocumentation Support is Required\n290\t\n291\tUXD Support is Required (RHOAIUX-7470 — visual treatment and content review)\n292\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8019f459d0512869","agent_id":"ac74c8e642485881c","session_id":"25d8375f-a217-4cdf-8fb9-81de62a8ecf8","uuid":"408d39c0-0d89-4370-bf00-077e9cc3eadb","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:31:43.266Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b8e6cfe1e608fec","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8002 decomposition"}
